WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:04.040
This is one of the Personal Rapid Transit
pods at London's Heathrow Airport.

00:00:04.040 --> 00:00:06.640
It'll take you from one of the car parks to
Terminal 5,

00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:09.720
and from Terminal 5 to one of the car parks.

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:10.791
And that's about it.

00:00:10.791 --> 00:00:12.430
When it was installed in 2011,

00:00:12.430 --> 00:00:14.139
oh, this was going to be the future of transport.

00:00:14.139 --> 00:00:17.460
But it hasn't really worked out that way.

00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:18.630
And more than that:

00:00:18.630 --> 00:00:22.169
they got beaten to the punch by 35 years.

00:00:22.169 --> 00:00:25.810
Because in 1975, West Virginia University
opened this:

00:00:25.810 --> 00:00:28.369
their own Personal Rapid Transit system.

00:00:28.369 --> 00:00:33.100
The pods here in the city of Morgantown might
not be quite as sleek and rounded...

00:00:33.100 --> 00:00:35.360
but there's 70 of them, they carry more people,

00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:38.430
and they go to five stations along an eight-mile
track.

00:00:38.430 --> 00:00:40.810
This really is personal transit: you push
a button,

00:00:40.810 --> 00:00:43.960
and a car arrives to take you where you want
to go, non-stop.

00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:47.370
At peak times, there'll be a car along every
few minutes

00:00:47.370 --> 00:00:48.950
for each separate destination.

00:00:48.950 --> 00:00:51.380
PRT never shuts down.

00:00:51.380 --> 00:00:54.210
We have run 40 years without any major incident.

00:00:54.210 --> 00:00:57.280
10 inches of snow and we’ll be fine.

00:00:57.280 --> 00:01:00.140
That’s because we had a heated track here.

00:01:00.140 --> 00:01:02.989
Kids don’t like it because we keep the school
open!

00:01:02.989 --> 00:01:06.049
There are no variables; if I’m going from
point A to point B,

00:01:06.049 --> 00:01:08.200
if it’s eight minutes, it is eight minutes.

00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:10.689
Back in the 1970s, the only option for getting
students

00:01:10.689 --> 00:01:15.399
between all the separate university campuses
here was a fleet of buses.

00:01:15.399 --> 00:01:18.590
But Morgantown is a city with steep hills
and narrow roads,

00:01:18.590 --> 00:01:20.170
so the result was gridlock.

00:01:20.170 --> 00:01:21.259
And at the time,

00:01:21.259 --> 00:01:25.389
American politicians were actually having
a bit of a craze for systems like this.

00:01:25.389 --> 00:01:28.479
Richard Nixon announced that there was development
money available,

00:01:28.479 --> 00:01:31.180
and West Virginia senator Robert Byrd,

00:01:31.180 --> 00:01:36.920
who was famous in part for just how much money
he managed to bring back to his own state,

00:01:36.920 --> 00:01:40.049
he managed to get Morgantown selected as the
testing site.

00:01:40.049 --> 00:01:43.240
The Boeing Company, and NASA's Jet Propulsion
Laboratory,

00:01:43.240 --> 00:01:45.590
were involved with getting the system to work.

00:01:45.590 --> 00:01:51.939
The system, right now, can carry well over
4000 passengers an hour.

00:01:51.939 --> 00:01:54.709
Every vehicle, when it departs station, has
a route.

00:01:54.709 --> 00:01:57.859
Every time an assigned vehicle moves over
a presence detector,

00:01:57.859 --> 00:02:00.859
that location is monitored by the station
electronics

00:02:00.859 --> 00:02:04.579
which then conveys that message back to the
central control room,

00:02:04.579 --> 00:02:09.220
and then that message is depicted by the mimic
board in central control.

00:02:09.220 --> 00:02:11.920
And each one of those blinking lights represents
a presence detector,

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:15.750
but it also represents
five seconds of travel time.

00:02:15.750 --> 00:02:20.250
In the train world, all the switching is done
on the track.

00:02:20.250 --> 00:02:22.460
So the track physically moves

00:02:22.460 --> 00:02:26.990
to orient a vehicle down a different pathway
on a different rail line.

00:02:26.990 --> 00:02:30.840
In our system, the vehicle switches on board.

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:31.840
The result of all that 1970s engineering...

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:37.150
well, it was massively over-budget and a little
rough around the edges to start,

00:02:37.150 --> 00:02:39.000
but that happens with prototypes.

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:41.530
The important part was: it worked.

00:02:41.530 --> 00:02:45.850
And it worked well enough to be expanded and
kept updated into the 21st century.

00:02:45.850 --> 00:02:48.840
Well, one of the problems we are having today

00:02:48.840 --> 00:02:51.840
is with obsolescence of parts for the vehicle,

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:56.070
as well for the electronics for the system.

00:02:56.070 --> 00:02:58.900
And back in the 70s, the Minuteman missile
system

00:02:58.900 --> 00:03:05.570
was the basis for the design of the electronics
for the system.

00:03:05.570 --> 00:03:07.560
And there’s a company called Thales,

00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:13.090
and they are here on site now upgrading the
electronics and the software for the system.

00:03:13.090 --> 00:03:18.870
The old system uses a little bit different
technology to run cars up and down the guideway.

00:03:18.870 --> 00:03:23.520
The cars do not know where they’re located
out on the guideway.

00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:27.400
In the new system, the Thales system, they
will know where they’re at.

00:03:27.400 --> 00:03:31.500
So that’s a big help when you’re trying
to ask a car where it’s at!

00:03:31.500 --> 00:03:34.880
The technical manual for the system is online
on WVU's web site,

00:03:34.880 --> 00:03:38.280
and it is 1970s engineering genius.

00:03:38.280 --> 00:03:41.280
Just like the pods at Heathrow decades later,

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:43.380
this was going to be the future of travel.

00:03:43.380 --> 00:03:44.840
So why wasn’t it?

00:03:44.840 --> 00:03:46.680
Why aren't systems like this everywhere?

00:03:46.680 --> 00:03:52.100
Well, in most cases you can do almost everything
that this can with regular light rail

00:03:52.100 --> 00:03:54.600
with less upfront cost and less political
risk.

00:03:54.600 --> 00:03:57.180
Sure, the PRT is a bit faster and a bit more efficient,

00:03:57.180 --> 00:03:58.830
and it's a bit more pleasant to ride,

00:03:58.830 --> 00:04:00.240
because every journey is non-stop --

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:03.150
but the rest of the world went with cheaper,

00:04:03.150 --> 00:04:06.940
simpler systems... just with much less charm.

00:04:06.940 --> 00:04:10.569
It’s the spine of our transportation network.

00:04:10.569 --> 00:04:13.100
It is also an icon for this institution.

00:04:13.100 --> 00:04:14.680
We do have some challenges,

00:04:14.680 --> 00:04:17.630
but we have an excellent crew, a dedicated,
passionate crew

00:04:17.630 --> 00:04:21.630
that just finds a way to make the system work
and keep going.

00:04:21.630 --> 00:04:24.010
It’s that little engine that could.

00:04:24.010 --> 00:04:28.410
Thank you to everyone at the Morgantown PRT
and West Virginia University.

00:04:28.410 --> 00:04:31.150
Pull down the description for more links about
them, and about the college!

